C++ Unary Minus Operator Overloading
Description

This C++ program demonstrates unary operator overloading by overloading the unary minus (-) operator for a Number class.

The operator-() function defines how the unary minus operator should work when it is applied to a Number object.

Class Used
1. Number Class

The Number class is used to store and display an integer value.

value stores the number.
The constructor initializes the value.
operator-() overloads the unary minus operator.
display() displays the stored value.
operator-() Function

The operator-() function performs the unary minus operation.

It takes no arguments because unary operators work on one operand.
It creates and returns a new Number object.
The stored value is changed to its negative value.

For example:

25 → -25
Main Function

In the main() function, a Number object is created with the value:

25

The unary minus operator is then applied:

Number second = -first;

The original and negated values are displayed.

Output
Original value: 25
Negated value: -25
OOP Concept Used

Unary Operator Overloading

Number
   |
   |-- value = 25
   |
   |-- operator-()
          ↓
       -25

The expression:

-first

internally calls:

first.operator-()

Thus, the program demonstrates how an operator can be given a specific meaning for a user-defined class.